odels these often a  good way to understand how components  should be used further you'll find  different building blocks that you need  to create models within a certain type  of domain in this case a one-dimensional  mechanical system the component package  contains masses Springs dampeners and  other things you need for this blow that  we have the sensor package these are  typically components that will read a  certain state in the model the position  of something the relative speed between  two parts and so on and convert it into  real valued signal while sister model  allows you to access every single  variable in your model off the  simulation using a sensor is a good way  to highlight the most important signals  it can also be used for feedback loops  and to to mimic real-world sensors such  as a speedometer the sources sub package  provides you with different ways to  interact with your models for example  you can set the position of a mechanical  part according to some input signal or  apply a force and so on the interface  package contains the parts needed to  build your own mechanical components or  whatever sub-layer you  looking at building your uncle aunt is  beyond the scope of this tutorial so we  want to be exploring it here looking at  the other packages available you'll see  that the general structure is present in  most of them some have additional  packages such as the media library here  for example in the thermal fluid heat  flow library but most have the examples  the components the sensors the sources  the interface since components might be  distributed across multiple packages if  you one want to find something in  particular a good way could be to search  for it say that we want to find a spring  we can type it in the search bar in the  class browser  turns out built into system ola there  are a lot of different types of springs  we can now narrow down the search by  using wildcards if we for example know  we're looking for a one-dimensional  spring we can start by typing in  translational to indicate the  translational library we were looking at  previously then we can use an asterisk  to indicate a wild-card search followed  by spring we found two types of spring  components here say we want to know more  about how we should use the spring where  it's selected in the class browser press  f1 on your keyboard to bring up the  documentation for the component I'll  scroll in a bit here for your benefit by  holding down ctrl on my keyboard and  using the scroll wheel  here you will find information on how to  use the component for example this  suggests that the spring should be put  between two sliding masses or between a  sliding mass and a a so-called fixed  component you can see descriptions of  the connectors on the component you can  also see descriptions of the different  parameters that you can set in the model  more importantly you will also find  cases where this class is used as a  component in other example models by  expanding the use in examples tab these  models will give you an understanding  for how the spring can be used  you'll also find other components where  the spring is used as a sub component  here you can see it as a part of a more  general three-dimensional spring click  on one on the models and you will enter  the model documentation  to view the diagram of the model click  the model name in the navigation bar the  model will open in the diagram view of  model Santa here we can locate the  spring in the diagram using the  information we've gathered so far I'll  create a simple model using the spring  first I'll switch back to the model I  had from the start then I'll drag the  spring into the diagram view of the  model  then I'll expand the translational  package again and add a fixed component  and a sliding mass and connect them all  together to make the model a bit more  interesting we can add something that  pushes on the mass like it did in this  pool with preload that 